{"book":{"id":"book-undocumented-lives-the-untold-story-of-mexican-migration-ana-raquel-minian","slug":"undocumented-lives-the-untold-story-of-mexican-migration-ana-raquel-minian","title":"Undocumented Lives: The Untold Story of Mexican Migration","authors":[{"id":"person-ana-raquel-minian","name":"Ana Raquel Minian"}],"publisherId":"publisher-harvard-university-press","imprintId":"imprint-harvard-university-press","isbn13":["9780674919969"],"subjects":["Society & Culture"],"topics":["Immigration, Refugees & Borderlands","Labor, Work & Organizing","Latin America & the Caribbean","Migration & Diaspora"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Undocumented%20Lives%3A%20The%20Untold%20Story%20of%20Mexican%20Migration%20Ana%20Raquel%20Minian","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=Undocumented%20Lives%3A%20The%20Untold%20Story%20of%20Mexican%20Migration%20Ana%20Raquel%20Minian","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=Undocumented%20Lives%3A%20The%20Untold%20Story%20of%20Mexican%20Migration%20Ana%20Raquel%20Minian","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Undocumented%20Lives%3A%20The%20Untold%20Story%20of%20Mexican%20Migration%20Ana%20Raquel%20Minian","publisher":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL20186400W"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-oah-frederick-jackson-turner-award-oah-frederick-jackson-turner-award-official-page-internet-arc","source-open-library-undocumented-lives-the-untold-story-of-mexican-migration-ana-raquel-minian","source-imprint-normalization","source-open-library-summary-undocumented-lives-the-untold-story-of-mexican-migration-ana-raquel-minian"],"publicationYear":2018,"thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8835013-L.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"academic","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.552,"traits":[{"id":"academic","score":0.53,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\"","academic press signal: Harvard University Press / Harvard University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.48,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\"","academic press signal: Harvard University Press / Harvard University Press"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":42,"criticalRenown":68,"controversy":18,"llmAffinity":78,"affinityResidual":0.49,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.78,"criticalRenown":0.78,"controversy":0.72,"llmAffinity":0.93},"tags":{"craft":"narrative-drive","evidence":"archival-research","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"summary":"In the 1970s the Mexican government acted to alleviate rural unemployment by supporting the migration of able-bodied men. Millions crossed into the United States to find work that would help them survive as well as sustain their families in Mexico. They took low-level positions that few Americans wanted and sent money back to communities that depended on their support. But as U.S. authorities pursued more aggressive anti-immigrant measures, migrants found themselves caught between the economic interests of competing governments. The fruits of their labor were needed in both places, and yet neither country made them feel welcome. Ana Raquel Minian explores this unique chapter in the history of Mexican migration. Undocumented Lives draws on private letters, songs, and oral testimony to recreate the experience of circular migration, which reshaped communities in the United States and Mexico. While migrants could earn for themselves and their families in the U.S., they needed to return to Mexico to reconnect with their homes periodically. Despite crossing the border many times, they managed to belong to communities on both sides of it. Ironically, the U.S. immigration crackdown of the mid-1980s disrupted these flows, forcing many migrants to remain north of the border permanently for fear of not being able to return to work.","subjectCategories":[